  <main class="main" id="main-content">
    <section class="section">
      <h2 class="section-title">What is the Bull vs Bear Sentiment Scorecard?</h2>
      <p class="section-body">Every trading day brings a flood of headlines, earnings reports, Fed commentary, and price swings — and it's genuinely hard to tell whether, on balance, today was a bullish day or a bearish one for US stocks. The Bull vs Bear Sentiment Scorecard solves this by having AI read the day's market-moving information and distill it into one simple number: a bullish-vs-bearish split, such as 60 vs 40.</p>
      <p class="section-body">The scorecard tracks major benchmarks and stocks including QQQ, SPY, TSLA, NVDA, and AAPL, and layers in macro context from the VIX volatility index, interest rates, and currency markets. Instead of a single opaque score, every factor behind the number is listed individually, so you can see exactly why the market leaned bullish or bearish that day.</p>
      <p class="section-body">It refreshes five times per trading day, giving you a running read on how sentiment evolves from the pre-market session through the close — without you needing to track five different news sites yourself.</p>
    </section>
    <section class="section">
      <h2 class="section-title">Why Use This Tool?</h2>
      <p class="section-body">Market narratives change fast, and it's easy to overweight the last headline you read. Having an AI systematically tally today's bullish and bearish factors — rather than relying on gut feeling or social media noise — gives you a more balanced, repeatable read on sentiment.</p>
      <p class="section-body">Because the scorecard updates five times a day, you can also see intraday sentiment shifts: for example, a market that opens bearish on weak jobs data but turns more bullish by the close on a dovish Fed comment. That kind of context is hard to get from a single end-of-day headline.</p>
    </section>
    <section class="section">
      <h2 class="section-title">How to Read the Scorecard</h2>
      <ol class="step-list">
        <li class="step-item">
          <span class="step-num">1</span>
          <div class="step-body"><strong class="step-name">Open the scorecard</strong><p class="step-text">Click Open Tool below. The page loads the latest bull-vs-bear split for that time slot — no login required.</p></div>
        </li>
        <li class="step-item">
          <span class="step-num">2</span>
          <div class="step-body"><strong class="step-name">Read the bull/bear split</strong><p class="step-text">The score shows what share of the day's market-moving factors are bullish versus bearish, for example 60 bullish vs 40 bearish, based on an AI reading of news, price action, and macro data.</p></div>
        </li>
        <li class="step-item">
          <span class="step-num">3</span>
          <div class="step-body"><strong class="step-name">Check the underlying factors</strong><p class="step-text">Below the score, each bullish and bearish factor is listed individually — earnings, Fed policy, rates, geopolitics, individual stock catalysts — so you can see exactly what is driving the score, not just the number.</p></div>
        </li>
        <li class="step-item">
          <span class="step-num">4</span>
          <div class="step-body"><strong class="step-name">Compare against the previous update</strong><p class="step-text">The scorecard refreshes five times per trading day. Comparing the current score to the prior update shows whether sentiment is improving or deteriorating intraday.</p></div>
        </li>
        <li class="step-item">
          <span class="step-num">5</span>
          <div class="step-body"><strong class="step-name">Use it as one input, not a signal to trade on</strong><p class="step-text">Sentiment scores describe the current mood — they are not a buy or sell signal on their own. Combine this with your own research and risk tolerance before making any decision.</p></div>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </section>
    <section class="section">
      <h2 class="section-title">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
      <div class="faq-list" itemscope itemtype="https://schema.org/FAQPage">
        <div class="faq-item" itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question">
          <h3 class="faq-q" itemprop="name">What is the Bull vs Bear Sentiment Scorecard?</h3>
          <div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer">
            <p class="faq-a" itemprop="text">It is a free tool that uses AI to read the day's US stock market news, price action, and macro data, then expresses the balance of bullish versus bearish factors as a simple split, such as 60 bullish vs 40 bearish.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="faq-item" itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question">
          <h3 class="faq-q" itemprop="name">How often does it update?</h3>
          <div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer">
            <p class="faq-a" itemprop="text">The scorecard is refreshed five times per US trading day, so you can see how sentiment shifts from the pre-market session through the market close.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="faq-item" itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question">
          <h3 class="faq-q" itemprop="name">Which stocks and data does it track?</h3>
          <div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer">
            <p class="faq-a" itemprop="text">It tracks major US stocks such as QQQ, SPY, TSLA, NVDA, and AAPL, alongside macro indicators including the VIX volatility index, interest rates, and currency (FX) moves.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="faq-item" itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question">
          <h3 class="faq-q" itemprop="name">Is this financial advice or a trading signal?</h3>
          <div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer">
            <p class="faq-a" itemprop="text">No. The scorecard summarizes market sentiment for informational purposes only. It is not a recommendation to buy or sell any security, and it should not replace your own research or a licensed financial advisor.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="faq-item" itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question">
          <h3 class="faq-q" itemprop="name">Do I need an account to use it?</h3>
          <div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer">
            <p class="faq-a" itemprop="text">No. The Bull vs Bear Sentiment Scorecard is completely free and requires no sign-up, no account, and no personal information of any kind.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
